{%- comment -%}
  Media with a max width.

  @param {number} [container_vw]        - the share of the viewport this block's CONTAINER
                                          occupies on desktop, as a plain number (a 6-of-12
                                          column slot = 50). Defaults to 100.
  @param {number} [container_vw_mobile] - same, below 768px. Defaults to 100.
  @param {string} [sizes]               - escape hatch, used verbatim, but ONLY when no max
                                          width is set. A pre-composed string can't be
                                          combined with this block's cap, so pass
                                          container_vw instead and let the block do the
                                          arithmetic.
  @param {string} [layout]
  @param {string} [loading]
  @param {string} [fetchpriority]

  Note: rendered inside _text-lockup via `{% render block %}`, none of these params are
  passed — the defaults below are what actually runs there.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% stylesheet %}
  /* Ratio mode only. With a ratio set, the image/video snippet positions the media
     `absolute`, so this box has NO in-flow content and nothing to derive a width from.
     Every _text-lockup alignment sets align-items to center/start/end — never stretch —
     so the block's own flex wrapper would shrink to fit that zero and the media collapses
     to a correctly-shaped sliver.

     Stretching the wrapper gives `w-full` on the box something real to resolve against.
     Scoped through :has() so it touches only ratio'd instances: .block-media is shared
     with the _media block, whose in-flow image still sizes itself the old way. Inert
     wherever the wrapper is not a flex item. */
  .block-media:has(> .has-aspect) {
    align-self: stretch;
  }
{% endstylesheet %}

{% capture space_below %}{% render 'util-block-spacing', space: block.settings.space_below %}{% endcapture %}

{%-liquid
  comment
    Aspect ratio. `nil`, not '' — an empty string is TRUTHY in Liquid, so passing one as
    `ratio` would send the image snippet down its ratio branch and divide by a zero aspect.
    nil is falsy there and leaves the image at its natural shape, which is what auto means.

    A chosen ratio also implies a fit: the media has to fill the box it is now cropped to,
    so layout defaults to cover — unless a caller passed its own, which still wins.
  endcomment
  assign media_aspect = nil
  unless block.settings.aspect_ratio == 'auto' or block.settings.aspect_ratio == blank
    capture media_aspect
      render 'util-aspect-ratio', ratio: block.settings.aspect_ratio
    endcapture
    assign media_aspect = media_aspect | strip
  endunless

  assign media_layout = layout
  if media_aspect != blank and media_layout == blank
    assign media_layout = 'cover'
  endif

  comment
    Max width is resolved per breakpoint, and each breakpoint carries its OWN unit,
    so a block can cap at a percentage on desktop and a fixed pixel width on mobile.
    The value is emitted WITH its unit — a bare number is not a valid max-width, so
    the px/% has to be appended here rather than in the CSS.

    The percent fields are sliders, so they always carry a value (100 = no cap).
    The pixel fields can be left blank: blank on desktop means "no cap" and falls
    through to 100%, and blank on mobile means "same as desktop", matching how
    header height and the grid margins already work.
  endcomment
  assign max_width = '100%'
  assign capped_desktop = false
  if block.settings.unit == 'pixel'
    if block.settings.max_pixels != blank
      assign max_width = block.settings.max_pixels | append: 'px'
      assign capped_desktop = true
    endif
  else
    if block.settings.max_percent != blank
      assign max_width = block.settings.max_percent | append: '%'
      if block.settings.max_percent < 100
        assign capped_desktop = true
      endif
    endif
  endif

  assign max_width_mobile = max_width
  assign capped_mobile = capped_desktop
  if block.settings.unit_mobile == 'pixel'
    if block.settings.max_pixels_mobile != blank
      assign max_width_mobile = block.settings.max_pixels_mobile | append: 'px'
      assign capped_mobile = true
    endif
  else
    if block.settings.max_percent_mobile != blank
      assign max_width_mobile = block.settings.max_percent_mobile | append: '%'
      assign capped_mobile = false
      if block.settings.max_percent_mobile < 100
        assign capped_mobile = true
      endif
    endif
  endif

  comment
    `sizes` has to describe the width the image will actually OCCUPY, which is the smaller
    of two things: the share of the viewport its container gets, and this block's own cap.
    Deriving it is the whole point of knowing the cap — left alone, a block capped at 400px
    still tells the browser to fetch a full-viewport image, and on a wide display that is
    several times the pixels needed.

    The container share is found in this order:
      1. a `container_vw` param, when a section renders this block directly and knows its
         own column share (media-w-text computes exactly this as media_vw);
      2. the COLUMNS section's own settings, read straight off `section` — this block is a
         grandchild there (columns → _text-lockup → here) and neither render passes params,
         so `section` is the only route to the divisor. Other blocks in this theme already
         read section this way (_media-slide, the PDP blocks);
      3. 100 — full width, the safe over-estimate.
  endcomment
  assign is_columns = false
  assign vw_desktop = container_vw | default: 100
  assign vw_tablet = vw_desktop
  assign vw_mobile = container_vw_mobile | default: 100

  comment
    100.0, not 100 — Liquid's divided_by does INTEGER division on two integers, so
    100 / 3 would floor to 33 and 100 / 6 to 16, quietly rounding the wrong way. Float
    division then ceil keeps every tier on the over-estimating side.
  endcomment
  if container_vw == blank and section.settings.columns != blank
    assign vw_desktop = 100.0 | divided_by: section.settings.columns | ceil
    assign vw_tablet = 100.0 | divided_by: section.settings.tablet_columns | ceil
    assign vw_mobile = 100.0 | divided_by: section.settings.mobile_columns | ceil
    assign is_columns = true
  endif

  comment
    Gutters and the page margin both make the real column NARROWER than 100/n, so ignoring
    them over-estimates — the safe direction. Under-estimating is what looks broken.
  endcomment
-%}

{%- liquid
  comment
    A percent cap resolves against the container, so it multiplies out to a plain vw and
    stays exact at every width. A pixel cap is an absolute length that neither dominates nor
    is dominated by the container share — 600px is the smaller of the two at 2560px wide and
    the larger at 800px — so it needs min() to stay correct at both ends.

    Rounding always goes UP: over-estimating costs a slightly larger file, under-estimating
    is visibly soft. They are not symmetric mistakes.
  endcomment
  assign pct_desktop = block.settings.max_percent | default: 100
  assign pct_mobile = block.settings.max_percent_mobile | default: 100
  assign px_desktop = block.settings.max_pixels
  assign px_mobile = block.settings.max_pixels_mobile
  assign use_px_desktop = false
  assign use_px_mobile = false
  if block.settings.unit == 'pixel' and px_desktop != blank
    assign use_px_desktop = true
    assign srcset_cap = px_desktop
  endif
  if block.settings.unit_mobile == 'pixel' and px_mobile != blank
    assign use_px_mobile = true
  endif

  comment
    Four entries, not two, because the two things being combined change at DIFFERENT
    breakpoints: the column count steps at 640 and 1024 (slideshow.js maps
    data-slides-to-show to --slides-xs/sm/md/lg), while this block's own cap steps at 768
    with its `md:` max-width. Merged, that is desktop-cap+desktop-cols above 1024,
    desktop-cap+tablet-cols from 768, mobile-cap+tablet-cols from 640, and
    mobile-cap+mobile-cols below. Outside the columns section the tiers collapse to the
    same value and the extra entries are harmless.
  endcomment
  if use_px_desktop
    assign size_lg = 'min(' | append: vw_desktop | append: 'vw, ' | append: px_desktop | append: 'px)'
    assign size_md = 'min(' | append: vw_tablet | append: 'vw, ' | append: px_desktop | append: 'px)'
  else
    assign size_lg = pct_desktop | times: vw_desktop | divided_by: 100.0 | ceil | append: 'vw'
    assign size_md = pct_desktop | times: vw_tablet | divided_by: 100.0 | ceil | append: 'vw'
  endif

  if use_px_mobile
    assign size_sm = 'min(' | append: vw_tablet | append: 'vw, ' | append: px_mobile | append: 'px)'
    assign size_xs = 'min(' | append: vw_mobile | append: 'vw, ' | append: px_mobile | append: 'px)'
  else
    assign size_sm = pct_mobile | times: vw_tablet | divided_by: 100.0 | ceil | append: 'vw'
    assign size_xs = pct_mobile | times: vw_mobile | divided_by: 100.0 | ceil | append: 'vw'
  endif

  if is_columns
    assign media_sizes = '(min-width: 1024px) ' | append: size_lg | append: ', (min-width: 768px) ' | append: size_md | append: ', (min-width: 640px) ' | append: size_sm | append: ', ' | append: size_xs
  else
    assign media_sizes = '(min-width: 768px) ' | append: size_lg | append: ', ' | append: size_xs
  endif

  comment
    Uncapped AND with no container knowledge, this block is a plain _media — a `sizes`
    string from the caller is then the most accurate thing available, so prefer it.
  endcomment
  unless capped_desktop or capped_mobile or is_columns
    if sizes != blank
      assign media_sizes = sizes
    endif
  endunless

  comment
    A pixel cap is also a hard ceiling on how many pixels can ever be USED, so the srcset
    can stop there. 3x covers the densest displays; candidates past that are dead weight in
    the HTML and a trap if `sizes` is ever wrong. A percent cap has no such ceiling — it
    grows with the viewport — so it deliberately leaves srcset_cap unset.
  endcomment
  if srcset_cap != blank
    assign srcset_cap = srcset_cap | times: 3
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  capped-desktop / capped-mobile are styling hooks for "a cap is actually in effect at this
  breakpoint" — not merely "a value is set". A percent cap of 100 and a blank pixel field
  both leave the media full width, so neither adds its class.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div
  class="media-wrapper max-w-(--media-max-mobile) md:max-w-(--media-max) {{ space_below | strip }}{% if capped_desktop %} capped-desktop{% endif %}{% if capped_mobile %} capped-mobile{% endif %}{% if media_aspect != blank %} relative w-full has-aspect aspect-(--media-aspect){% endif %}"
  style="--media-max: {{ max_width }}; --media-max-mobile: {{ max_width_mobile }};{% if media_aspect != blank %} --media-aspect: {{ media_aspect }};{% endif %}"
>
  {% if block.settings.media_type == 'video' %}
    {% if block.settings.video != blank %}
      {% render 'video',
        src: block.settings.video,
        layout: media_layout
      %}
    {% endif %}
  {% else %}
    {% if block.settings.image != blank %}
      {% render 'image',
        src: block.settings.image,
        ratio: media_aspect,
        layout: media_layout,
        loading: loading,
        fetchpriority: fetchpriority,
        sizes: media_sizes,
        max_width: srcset_cap
      %}
    {% endif %}
  {% endif %}
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Media",
  "class": "block-media relative",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "media_type",
      "default": "image",
      "label": "Media Type",
      "options": [
        {
          "label": "Image",
          "value": "image"
        },
        {
          "label": "Video",
          "value": "video"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "image_picker",
      "id": "image",
      "label": "Image",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.media_type == 'image' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "video",
      "id": "video",
      "label": "Video",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.media_type == 'video' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "aspect_ratio",
      "default": "auto",
      "label": "Aspect Ratio",
      "info": "Auto keeps the media's own shape. Any other value crops it to that shape.",
      "options": [
        { "label": "Auto", "value": "auto" },
        { "label": "Portrait (2:3)", "value": "portrait_2_3" },
        { "label": "Portrait (3:4)", "value": "portrait_3_4" },
        { "label": "Portrait (4:5)", "value": "portrait_4_5" },
        { "label": "Square (1:1)", "value": "square" },
        { "label": "Landscape (5:4)", "value": "landscape_5_4" },
        { "label": "Landscape (4:3)", "value": "landscape_4_3" },
        { "label": "Landscape (3:2)", "value": "landscape_3_2" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Max Width — Desktop"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "unit",
      "default": "percent",
      "label": "Unit",
      "options": [
        {
          "label": "Percent",
          "value": "percent"
        },
        {
          "label": "Pixels",
          "value": "pixel"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "max_percent",
      "label": "Max width",
      "min": 1,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 1,
      "unit": "%",
      "default": 100,
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.unit == 'percent' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "number",
      "id": "max_pixels",
      "label": "Max width (px)",
      "info": "Leave blank for no limit.",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.unit == 'pixel' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Max Width — Mobile"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "unit_mobile",
      "default": "percent",
      "label": "Unit",
      "options": [
        {
          "label": "Percent",
          "value": "percent"
        },
        {
          "label": "Pixels",
          "value": "pixel"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "max_percent_mobile",
      "label": "Max width",
      "min": 1,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 1,
      "unit": "%",
      "default": 100,
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.unit_mobile == 'percent' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "number",
      "id": "max_pixels_mobile",
      "label": "Max width (px)",
      "info": "Leave blank to use the desktop value.",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.unit_mobile == 'pixel' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Spacing"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "space_below",
      "label": "Space Below",
      "default": "none",
      "options": [
        { "label": "None", "value": "none" },
        { "label": "Small", "value": "small" },
        { "label": "Medium", "value": "medium" },
        { "label": "Large", "value": "large" },
        { "label": "XL", "value": "xl" }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "blocks": [],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Media"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
